pouch boundary-line patch 0069 - SIGUSR1 and SIGUSR2 are the `usr1` and `usr2` notes.

Operator vote 15 (2026-11-02). The kernel has both notes now (NOTE_BIT_USR1 = 8,
NOTE_BIT_USR2 = 9, default terminate, a death recorded as the signal; docs/ERRORS.md
"The user-defined notes"). Before this sigaction, kill, raise and pthread_kill all
answered EINVAL for them, and pthread_kill(t, SIGUSR1) is the classic use of naming a
thread.

  src/internal/_pouch_signal.h  POUCH_NOTE_BIT_USR1 and POUCH_NOTE_BIT_USR2, and the
      supported mask 0xaf -> 0x3af, so __block_all_sigs and __block_app_sigs block them.
  src/signal/_pouch_signal.c    __pouch_sig_to_note names them; the sigset translations
      give each its own bit both ways; the handler dispatches each name to its signal.
      An uncaught one reaches SYS_NOTED(NDFLT), whose default for it is terminate.
  src/signal/sigaction.c        Both join the supported set.

A signal with no note is still EINVAL; SIGALRM is the example now.

Witnesses: pouch-hello-signals `usr-apart` and `usr-death`, pouch-hello-threads
`kill-usr`.

--- a/src/internal/_pouch_signal.h
+++ b/src/internal/_pouch_signal.h
@@ -99,7 +99,11 @@
 /* SIGTERM's own note, `term` (NOTE_BIT_TERM): masked and caught apart
  * from SIGINT's `interrupt`. */
 #define POUCH_NOTE_BIT_TERM        (1u << 7)
-#define POUCH_NOTE_MASK_SUPPORTED  0xafu
+/* SIGUSR1's and SIGUSR2's notes, `usr1` and `usr2` (NOTE_BIT_USR1 and
+ * NOTE_BIT_USR2): a bit each, default terminate. */
+#define POUCH_NOTE_BIT_USR1        (1u << 8)
+#define POUCH_NOTE_BIT_USR2        (1u << 9)
+#define POUCH_NOTE_MASK_SUPPORTED  0x3afu
 
 /* SYS_NOTED arg semantics (matches kernel/include/thylacine/notes.h
  * "SYS_NOTED arg semantics" docblock, R4-F6 audit close). */
